      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village was named in honor of James Cochran, reflecting his role as an early community leader in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cochranville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Fagg's Manor Presbyterian Church, organized in 1739, kept session records of memberships, baptisms, and marriages through 1906.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cochranville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "While Cochranville lacked a major rail station, proximity to Pennsylvania Railroad branches let farmers reach broader markets by the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cochranville_pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 120‑acre Cochran family farm, now preserved under the Agricultural Land Preservation Program, still supports hay, corn, wheat, and horse breeding.",
